Is there an R package that can do simultaneous regression equations such that I can determine how well each IV predicts each DV (classic "horizontal" comparisons) and that allows me to assess each IVs differential influence on each DV ("vertical" comparisons)?

Simultaneous regression Equations:
DVs Modifying IV IV IV IV IV IV IV IV IV IV IV IV
RT x1 F1 F2 F3 S1 S2 S3 E1 E2 E3 E4 E5
Error rate 1 x2 F1 F2 F3 S1 S2 S3 E1 E2 E3 E4 E5
Error rate 2 x3 F1 F2 F3 S1 S2 S3 E1 E2 E3 E4 E5
Error rate 3 x4 F1 F2 F3 S1 S2 S3 E1 E2 E3 E4 E5
Error rate 4 x5 F1 F2 F3 S1 S2 S3 E1 E2 E3 E4 E5
Error rate 5 x6 F1 F2 F3 S1 S2 S3 E1 E2 E3 E4 E5
Q1 How well does each IV predict each DV?
Q2 What is the differential influence of each IV on Each DV (e.g., Does IV E2 predict Error rate 3 better than Error rate 4)?

Information about my data:

DV Type
RT continuous
Error rate 1 proportion
Error rate 2 proportion
Error rate 3 proportion
Error rate 4 proportion
Error rate 5 proportion
* Note that Error rates 2-5 are mutually exclusive subsets of error rate 1
Error rate 1 Overall error rate
Error rate 2 Rate of error type A
Error rate 3 Rate of error type B
Error rate 4 Rate of error type C
Error rate 5 Rate of error type D
IV Type
x1 count modifier
F1 continuous
F2 continuous
F3 continuous
S1 ratio
S2 count
S3 count
E1 count
E2 count
E3 count
E4 count
E5 continuous

** Note that all distributions - both DVs and IVs are non-normal**

Correlations between IVs and modifier
x1
F1 -0.340
F2 0.705
F3 0.917
S1 0.882
S2 0.634
S3 0.694
E1 0.744
E2 0.638
E3 0.244
E4 0.228
E5 0.899

If R cannot do this analysis but Mplus can, can someone please explain what it is about Mplus that allows it to do what R cannot?

